    "confidence": 0.5,
    "explanation": "To both parties: this case cannot be decided in money, and I am sending it to a human reviewer under POL-RD-10(5). Here is why, and what I found on the way.\n\nThe deviation itself is real and is established. Measured as POL-RD-01 requires — on gps_trace.trace_distance_km, not the trip record's rounded convenience copy of 17.1 km — the drive was 17.08 km against the booked optimal route of 13.9 km. The excess is 3.18 km, or 22.9% of optimal. That clears both establishment gates (more than 0.8 km and more than 20%), so the rider's core measurement is accepted. It does not reach either materiality gate (8.0 km or 50%), so no POL-RD-06 pattern machinery is engaged.\n\nWhat stops a money ruling is the driver's explanation. He says a bus breakdown blocked the main road and forced him via Balestier. That is a road-closure excuse, and this policy can only accept or reject it against a traffic_advisory. There is no advisory on this record: EV-ADVIS-00530-01 is a missing artifact — status MISSING, weight_multiplier 0.0, issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT (BLOCKING). Under POL-GEN-02(3) I name it: it is at weight 0.0 and can carry no fact, and its absence is itself citable. POL-RD-10(5) makes 'a closure asserted with no traffic_advisory' a mandatory escalation, and it is not answered by POL-RD-08's burden rule — POL-RD-08 decides whose assertion fails, it does not decide whether a human should look at a closure claim the platform's own advisory data cannot confirm. ESCALATE is terminal: the disputed SGD 26.95 is held, not collected and not refunded.\n\nSo nothing is lost, I record the arithmetic both of you argued over. Had the matter been ruled on the deviation, POL-RD-02 (the fare model is METERED, so POL-RD-11 does not apply) would have given: charged_total 26.95; recomputed_fare 21.185 = (3.80 base + 0.75 x 13.9 km + 0.28 x 22 min = 20.385) x the fare_breakdown surge multiplier of 1.0 + 0.80 platform fee; refund 5.765, rounded up to SGD 5.80 payable. That is 21.4% of the charge — a PARTIAL_REFUND, not the full SGD 26.95 the rider seeks. The rider's demand for the whole fare is rejected on that arithmetic, and the driver's tier argument ('a taxi meter is dearer than the discount app cars') is no defence under any clause: the corridor the driver may drive is the platform's optimal route for the booked product, not another product's price. The rider's 'around fifty dollars / seventy-six dollars' narrative names no other trip and cites no other artifact, so it carries no weight under POL-RD-08; the only deviation measure is this trip's own trace. No POL-RD-06 conduct referral and no POL-RD-02 full-refund uplift arise: 22.9% is below the materiality gate and the driver's trailing 90-day counters (0 deviation flags over 250 completed trips) show no pattern.\n\nConfidence is 0.5, below the 0.70 platform floor. The disposition turns on an artifact that is absent at weight 0.0, and the only support for the driver's closure account is an unweighted counterparty statement; the deviation evidence itself is clean at weight 1.0, but it is not what decides this outcome.\n\nWhat would resolve this for the reviewer: a traffic_advisory naming a road on the optimal corridor with a validity window overlapping this trip, or a platform record or driver admission establishing whether the road was in fact blocked. With that, the case can be decided on POL-RD-03 and POL-RD-02.",
    "referrals": []
  },
